## What Is Inheritance?

Inheritance is an object-oriented programming mechanism that allows a new class to derive from an existing class and reuse or specialize its behavior.

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User(this.name);

  void login() {
    print('$name logged in');
  }
}

class AdminUser extends User {
  AdminUser(super.name);

  void manageUsers() {
    print('$name is managing users');
  }
}
```

The relationship is `AdminUser is a User`.

## Why Does Inheritance Exist?

Inheritance is useful when there is a genuine is-a relationship.

For example:

```text
AdminUser is a User
Dog is an Animal
Car is a Vehicle
```

Do not use inheritance merely because one class needs some methods from another class.

A relationship such as:

```text
Car has an Engine
```

is composition rather than inheritance.

## Dart's Inheritance Model

Dart uses single class inheritance. A class has one superclass, although Dart provides other mechanisms such as mixins and interfaces for different forms of reuse and type relationships.

For this lesson, the focus is class inheritance through `extends`.

```dart
class Animal {}

class Dog extends Animal {}
```

The inheritance chain can contain multiple levels:

```text
Animal
  ↑
Mammal
  ↑
Dog
```

But each class still has only one direct superclass.

## Types of Inheritance

Inheritance terminology is often taught using a language-independent OOP taxonomy. We need to distinguish that terminology from what Dart directly supports.

### Single Inheritance

One child class inherits from one parent class.

```text
Animal
  ↑
Dog
```

```dart
class Animal {}

class Dog extends Animal {}
```

Dart supports this directly.

### Multilevel Inheritance

A class derives from a class that itself derives from another class.

```text
Animal
  ↑
Mammal
  ↑
Dog
```

```dart
class Animal {}

class Mammal extends Animal {}

class Dog extends Mammal {}
```

Dart supports this because each class still has one superclass.

### Hierarchical Inheritance

Multiple child classes share the same parent.

```text
        Animal
        /    \
       /      \
     Dog      Cat
```

```dart
class Animal {}

class Dog extends Animal {}

class Cat extends Animal {}
```

Dart supports this structure.

### Multiple Inheritance

Multiple inheritance means that one class has multiple superclass parents.

Conceptually:

```text
Animal       Flyer
    \         /
     \       /
       Bird
```

Dart does not support multiple superclass inheritance. A Dart class has one superclass.

### Hybrid Inheritance

Hybrid inheritance combines multiple inheritance structures, such as hierarchical and multilevel inheritance.

Dart does not support arbitrary hybrid inheritance through multiple superclasses because Dart does not support multiple superclass inheritance.

Other Dart mechanisms such as mixins, interfaces, and composition can express different forms of reuse or relationships, but they should not be described as multiple superclass inheritance.

## Inheritance Type Decision Table

| Inheritance Type | Dart Support | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Single | Supported | One child has one superclass |
| Multilevel | Supported | Inheritance forms a chain |
| Hierarchical | Supported | Multiple children share a superclass |
| Multiple superclass inheritance | Not supported | One class has multiple superclasses |
| Hybrid through multiple superclasses | Not supported | Combination involving multiple superclass inheritance |

This distinction is important in interviews. Saying that Dart supports multiple inheritance simply because it supports interfaces or mixins is technically incorrect.

## Basic `extends`

Dart uses `extends` to establish class inheritance.

```dart
class Animal {
  void eat() {
    print('Eating');
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  void bark() {
    print('Barking');
  }
}
```

The `Dog` object can use both the inherited `eat()` method and its own `bark()` method.

## What Does the Child Inherit?

A subclass can inherit accessible instance members from its superclass, subject to Dart's language and library rules.

Inheritance establishes a subtype relationship rather than copying source code.

## Inheritance Does Not Mean Copying Code

Inheritance establishes a type relationship. It does not simply copy the parent's source code into the child.

```dart
Vehicle vehicle = Car('Example');
```

A `Car` can be treated as a `Vehicle` because it is a subtype of `Vehicle`.

This relationship becomes the foundation for polymorphism.

## The `super` Keyword

`super` is used to access the superclass context, commonly to invoke superclass constructors or methods.

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User(this.name);

  void login() {
    print('Login');
  }
}

class AdminUser extends User {
  AdminUser(super.name);

  void openDashboard() {
    super.login();
    print('Opening dashboard');
  }
}
```

## Calling a Superclass Constructor

A subclass defines its own constructors and initializes the superclass appropriately.

```dart
class User {
  final String name;

  User(this.name);
}

class AdminUser extends User {
  AdminUser(String name) : super(name);
}
```

Modern Dart also supports super parameters:

```dart
class AdminUser extends User {
  AdminUser(super.name);
}
```

Dart's current documentation confirms that constructors are not inherited and that subclasses initialize the superclass through constructor invocation or super parameters.

## Constructors Are Not Inherited

Constructors are not inherited like ordinary instance members.

A subclass defines its own constructors and initializes the superclass when required.

## Method Overriding

When a child class provides a new implementation for an inherited member, it is overriding that member.

```dart
class Animal {
  void makeSound() {
    print('Some sound');
  }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
  @override
  void makeSound() {
    print('Bark');
  }
}
```

Dart allows subclasses to override inherited instance methods, getters, and setters.

## Why `@override`?

`@override` documents the programmer's intention and allows Dart's analyzer to detect mistakes in the override declaration.

## Override vs New Method

If a child replaces inherited behavior, it is overriding:

```dart
@override
void eat() {}
```

If the child introduces behavior that did not exist in the parent, it is a new member:

```dart
void bark() {}
```

## Why Override Behavior?

Different subclasses can expose the same operation while implementing it differently.

```dart
class Notification {
  void send() {
    print('Sending notification');
  }
}

class EmailNotification extends Notification {
  @override
  void send() {
    print('Sending email');
  }
}

class PushNotification extends Notification {
  @override
  void send() {
    print('Sending push notification');
  }
}
```

All notification types provide `send()`, but each implementation can behave differently.

## Polymorphism

Polymorphism is the capability of different object types to be treated as instances of a common parent class or interface, executing different behaviors through the exact same method call. Derived from Greek meaning `many forms`, it allows different concrete objects to be used through a common parent type while providing specialized runtime behavior.

```dart
final Notification notification = EmailNotification();
notification.send();
```

The variable has the static type `Notification`, while the runtime object is an `EmailNotification`.

Therefore the overridden `EmailNotification.send()` implementation executes.

## Static Type vs Runtime Object Type

Consider:

```dart
Notification notification = EmailNotification();
```

The declared/static type is `Notification`.

The runtime object type is `EmailNotification`.

This distinction allows code to work against a common parent type while runtime behavior comes from the actual object.

## Real-World Example — Payment Methods

Suppose an application supports different payment methods.

```dart
class PaymentMethod {
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Processing payment: $amount');
  }
}

class CardPayment extends PaymentMethod {
  @override
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Processing card payment: $amount');
  }
}

class WalletPayment extends PaymentMethod {
  @override
  void pay(double amount) {
    print('Processing wallet payment: $amount');
  }
}
```

A checkout service can work with the parent type:

```dart
void processPayment(
  PaymentMethod paymentMethod,
  double amount,
) {
  paymentMethod.pay(amount);
}
```

The caller does not need to inspect the concrete payment class simply to decide which implementation to call.

This demonstrates polymorphism without introducing abstract classes or interfaces. Those topics belong to the next lesson.

## Upcasting

Assigning a child object to a parent-type variable is commonly called upcasting.

```dart
final Animal animal = Dog();
```

This is safe because every `Dog` is an `Animal`.

## Downcasting

If a value is known to contain a more specific object, Dart provides `is` and `as` for type checks and casts.

```dart
if (animal is Dog) {
  animal.bark();
}
```

Or:

```dart
final dog = animal as Dog;
dog.bark();
```

`as` should be used carefully because an incompatible runtime object can cause a cast failure.

## `is` vs `as`

`is` checks whether a value has a particular runtime type and can enable type promotion.

`as` performs an explicit cast to a target type and can fail when the runtime value is incompatible.

## Polymorphism With Collections

Different concrete implementations can be stored in a collection of their common parent type.

```dart
final notifications = <Notification>[
  EmailNotification(),
  PushNotification(),
];

for (final notification in notifications) {
  notification.send();
}
```

The loop does not need to know the concrete notification type.

## `super` vs `this`

`this` refers to the current object.

`super` refers to the superclass context.

Think:

```text
this
↓
current object

super
↓
superclass context
```

## Inheritance vs Composition

Inheritance represents an `is-a` relationship.

```text
AdminUser is a User
```

Composition represents a `has-a` or `uses-a` relationship.

```text
Car has an Engine
```

A composition implementation might be:

```dart
class Car {
  final Engine engine;

  Car(this.engine);
}
```

## Why Composition Can Be Better

If a repository merely uses an API service, it does not necessarily mean that the repository is an API service.

Instead of:

```dart
class ProductRepository extends ApiService {}
```

composition can express the relationship more accurately:

```dart
class ProductRepository {
  final ApiService apiService;

  ProductRepository(this.apiService);
}
```

## Inheritance and Type Safety

If a variable is typed as the parent:

```dart
Animal animal = Dog();
```

code can use members available through `Animal`. It cannot assume every `Animal` is a `Dog`.

Therefore:

```dart
animal.bark();
```

is not generally available when `animal` is statically typed as `Animal`.

## `Overriding` and `super`

A subclass can override behavior while still using the parent implementation.

```dart
class User {
  void login() {
    print('Perform common login');
  }
}

class AdminUser extends User {
  @override
  void login() {
    super.login();
    print('Load admin permissions');
  }
}
```

This is useful when the child needs to extend rather than completely replace the parent behavior.

## What Can Be Overridden?

For normal Dart classes, inherited instance members can be overridden where the language rules permit.

Typical examples include:

- methods
- getters
- setters

```dart
class User {
  String get role => 'user';
}

class AdminUser extends User {
  @override
  String get role => 'admin';
}
```

Dart's current documentation explicitly lists instance methods, getters, and setters as members that subclasses can override.

## Method Signature Compatibility

An override must remain compatible with the member being overridden.

```dart
class Animal {
  void eat(String food) {}
}
```

A child cannot arbitrarily change the contract to:

```dart
@override
void eat(int food) {}
```

That would not represent a compatible override.

Polymorphism depends on the subtype remaining usable where the parent type is expected.

